 crude  petroleum and natural gas account for  about 35 percent of Saudi Arabia's GDP  now following the Abraham Accords  Bahrain is working to attract more  investment from the U.S Israel and  Beyond as it works to continue growing  its non-oil sectors during a rare visit  to the Royal Palace which we could  photograph but not film the country's  Finance Minister Sheikh Salman binhalifa  al-halifa said Bahrain has what he calls  small country Advantage which has helped  the kingdom move quickly on economic  reforms Bahrain has also been making an  effort to boost tourism and capitalize  on being an island with water in the  Middle East to increase the number of  nights that tourists stay and the amount  of money that they spend the country has  opened a billion dollar International  Airport the gulf's largest Exhibition  Center and a new Amphitheater near its  existing Formula One Track our  population is one and a half million if  we welcome 12 14 million tourists a year  that's significant but Bahrain faces  challenges close by the country is off  Often overshadowed by its larger  neighbors who have built flashy Islands  announced futuristic cities and hosted  massive sporting events that includes  Qatar which Bahrain has icy relations  with and perhaps most critically  neighboring Saudi Arabia has begun to  liberalize allowing music festivals  movies and possibly even wine bars  Bahrain in Saudi are linked by this  Causeway which is just 15 miles long  it's bahrain's only land connection to  the outside world and currently a  majority of bahrain's tourists come from  Saudi to enjoy things that aren't  allowed back home like alcohol as Saudi  liberalizes will they still be coming to  Bahrain we're confident that growth and  development is Saudi is a benefit for  all of us in this region what really  happens is the pie becomes bigger and  everybody's individual slice of the pie  continues to get bigger  foreign

